<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cotys I (Odrysian)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. 384–360 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cotys I was king of the Odrysian kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a> from 384 to 360 BCE. He strengthened royal power over the Thracian tribes and fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> for control of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallipoli">Thracian Chersonese</a>, although he had married his daughter to the Athenian general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iphicrates">Iphicrates</a>. He was murdered in 360 BCE, after which his kingdom was divided among rival heirs, opening Thrace to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-macedon">Philip II of Macedon</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Cotys was born during the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seuthes-i">Seuthes I</a>. An Athenian inscription of 330 BCE honours Reboulas, brother of Cotys and son of a King Seuthes, and scholars who once took his father to be <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seuthes-ii">Seuthes II</a> now hold it was Seuthes I, since Seuthes II was only seven when Seuthes I abdicated in 411 BCE. His standing before he became king is obscure, but he is tentatively identified as a brother of Hebryzelmis, the Odrysian king honoured by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> in 386/385 BCE. Around 390 BCE the Athenian general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iphicrates">Iphicrates</a>, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a> to make peace between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amadocus-i">Amadocus I</a> and Seuthes II, married a daughter of Cotys in opulent circumstances.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Cotys may have ruled at first in the interior. With the help of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iphicrates">Iphicrates</a> he established himself as the heir of Hebryzelmis and removed rivals, possibly including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amadocus-ii">Amadocus II</a> and Saratocus. Inscribed objects found to the north and west, among them pieces of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rogozen-treasure">Rogozen Treasure</a> that bear his name, suggest an expansion the written sources do not record. His push towards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> and the Chersonese alarmed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, and in the early 370s BCE cities joined the Second Athenian Confederacy partly as a safeguard against him.</p><p>In 365 BCE he welcomed back <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iphicrates">Iphicrates</a>, now dismissed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenary</a> commander Charidemus, who married another daughter. His general Miltokythes starved Sestos into surrender in 363 or 362 BCE, then rebelled, seizing the royal treasury at Hieron Oros. Athens backed Miltokythes, but Cotys, aided by Iphicrates and Charidemus, defeated its fleet and forced him out in spring 361 BCE; according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demosthenes">Demosthenes</a>, they bribed the Athenian commanders. By 360 BCE Cotys held almost all of the Chersonese.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In September 360 BCE Python and Heraclides of Aenus, two students of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a> thought close to the king, killed Cotys at a feast in his palace, claiming he had wronged their father. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> made them honorary citizens and gave them gold wreaths. Some authors put his death in 359 BCE, but the arrival of the Athenian commander Cephisodotus in 360 BCE makes the earlier date certain.</p><p>Cotys was the last ruler of a relatively unified <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a> for centuries. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cersobleptes">Cersobleptes</a> had to share the kingdom with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amadocus-ii">Amadocus II</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berisades">Berisades</a>. Because Cotys turned from ally of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> to dangerous foe, the sources, above all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demosthenes">Demosthenes</a>, depict him as drunken, cruel and perfidious, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> tells of him breaking a gift of fine pottery so that his temper would not lead him to punish servants who broke it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cotys I (Odrysian)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
